(project borge-history
 (doc "Git is the history engine. Every agent action gets a commit.")
 (status implemented)
 (exports log_commit log_action show_history)
 (section philosophy
  (doc "Why git IS the history system")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The original design had a separate .borg-history/ git repo for
        checkpoints. That was over-engineering. The project already has
        git. Every agent change gets a commit. The commit IS the
        checkpoint. The commit message IS the log. Git diff IS the
        semantic diff engine.

        What makes borge history different from plain git is that borge
        understands the structure of .borg files. It can give you a
        semantic diff (status transitions, section additions, comment
        changes) instead of a text diff. But the storage is still git.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section agent-commit-discipline
  (doc "Agent must commit after every action")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Every agent action ends with a git commit. No exceptions. This
        is what makes the audit trail work. If the agent does not
        commit, that is a process violation.

        The commit workflow:
        1. Agent makes changes
        2. Agent runs borge balance + borge check
        3. Agent git adds relevant files
        4. Agent git commits with a descriptive message
        5. Agent runs worktree check (see below)

        If balance or check fails, the agent fixes before committing.
        The commit must never leave the worktree in a broken state.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section worktree-check
  (doc "Verify the worktree is clean and right before declaring done")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Two levels of worktree verification:

        Deterministic (borge check --worktree):
          - git diff is clean (no uncommitted changes)
          - borge balance passes on all .borg files
          - borge check passes on all .borg files
          - dune build succeeds
          - dune runtest passes
          This is pass/fail. CI-friendly. No judgment calls.

        Non-deterministic (borge review):
          - borge lint: any human comment deletions?
          - borge drift: any undocumented functions?
          - Does the commit match what the agent claimed to do?
          - Are there orphaned .borg files?
          This is advisory. It surfaces information for human review.
          It does not pass or fail — it informs.

        Agents run the deterministic check at the end of every action.
        Humans run the non-deterministic review when they want to
        understand what changed.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section semantic-operations
  (doc "Git operations that understand borge structure")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        borge log: git log but with borge-aware summaries.
          Shows only commits that modified .borg files.

        borge diff: git diff for .borg files.
          Highlights status transitions and section changes.

        borge undo: git revert HEAD.
          Convenience command. Current state is preserved because
          revert creates a new commit, not a destructive operation.

        All of these are git operations that understand the borge
        model. No separate storage engine needed.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section session-reports
  (doc "Structured HTML output for every agent session")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Every borge command that opens a harness (make, plan,
        drift --agent) generates a structured HTML session report
        on commit. The report is a committed artifact in the repo,
        not a separate database. This makes agent sessions:
        - Auditable without digging through .borge.lock/ before deletion
        - Browsable by clicking through .borge-history/
        - Renderable by victor (iframe or hx-get) and brochure
          (as part of the public site)
        - Persistent — they live in git, not in a database

        The report replaces the need for victor's job queue output
        storage. Instead of streaming agent output to a PostgreSQL
        table, borge commits the report as HTML. Victor reads it.
        This is a major architectural simplification: borge owns
        the content, victor and brochure are just viewers.
      |))
  )
  (subsection report-generation
   (doc "How session reports are generated during Lock.teardown")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         On borge commit, before removing .borge.lock/:

         1. Read .borge.lock/prompt.md — what was the agent asked
         2. Read .borge.lock/journal — what the agent claimed to do
         3. Read .borge.lock/role — implementer / spec-writer / observer
         4. Read .borge.lock/state — completed / failed
         5. Run git diff HEAD — what actually changed in code
         6. Parse .borg status transitions (planned → implemented)
         7. Generate .borge-history/session-{timestamp}.html
         8. Generate/update .borge-history/index.html
            (reverse chronological, newest first)
         9. Git add .borge-history/ with the rest of the commit
        10. THEN remove .borge.lock/

         The report is pure static HTML with embedded CSS.
         No JavaScript framework. No external dependencies.
         The CSS uses the same variable set as victor so the
         visual language is consistent.

         lib/lock/session_report.ml is the generator module.
         Called from Lock.teardown after integrity check passes.
        |))))
  (subsection report-structure
   (doc "What each session report HTML page contains")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Each session-{timestamp}.html contains two views,
         switchable via a tab or toggle:

         VIEW 1: DIFF (default)
         Shows what changed in the codebase:
         - File list with +/- line counts
         - Expandable per-file diffs
         - Semantic highlights for .borg files:
           Status transitions (planned → implemented) in green
           New spec sections in blue
           Deleted human comments in red
           New agent comments in yellow
         - Standard diff coloring for code files
         - Summary: N files changed, N insertions, N deletions

         VIEW 2: FULL SESSION
         Shows the complete agent session:
         - Role badge (Implementer / Spec-writer / Observer)
         - Prompt summary (first N lines, click to expand full)
         - Full journal entries (timestamped, structured)
         - State: Completed / Failed
         - Time elapsed (from lock creation to teardown)
         - Files touched summary table
         - Confirmed: integrity check passed / failed

         Both views are in a single HTML file with CSS-driven
         tab switching. No JS required for basic functionality.
        |))))
  (subsection report-index
   (doc "The .borge-history/index.html session browser")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         .borge-history/index.html lists all sessions in reverse
         chronological order. Each entry shows:
         - Timestamp
         - Role badge
         - Session summary (first line of journal or prompt)
         - File count and +/- summary
         - Status (completed / failed)

         Clicking an entry navigates to session-{timestamp}.html.
         The index defaults to highlighting the latest session.

         borge history:
           Opens file:// or launches a simple HTTP server
           pointing at .borge-history/index.html.
           If $BROWSER is set, opens in browser.
           Otherwise, prints the path.

         borge history --serve [--port 8080]:
           Starts python3 -m http.server (or similar) at
           .borge-history/ for interactive browsing.
        |))))
  (subsection diff-rendering
   (doc "Semantic diff rendering for .borg files")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The diff view in session reports uses the same semantic
         awareness as borge diff:
         - Status transitions: (status planned) → (status implemented)
           rendered as highlighted green with a transition arrow
         - New sections: (section ...) additions in blue
         - Deleted human comments: red with warning icon
         - New agent comments: yellow with agent icon
         - Plain text diff: standard +/- coloring

         The diff renderer is a function:
           git_diff_output → structured_html

         It does NOT use x-ocaml (which is for embedding
         executable OCaml in web pages). Diff rendering is
         pure server-side HTML generation. x-ocaml could be
         used later for interactive code examples in reports,
         but is NOT needed for diff rendering.

         For code file diffs (.ml, .go, .c, etc.), the renderer
         provides standard unified diff format with line numbers
         and syntax-aware highlighting where available.
        |))))
  (subsection victor-integration
   (doc "How victor consumes session reports instead of job queue output")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         With session reports in borge, victor's architecture
         simplifies dramatically:

         BEFORE (current):
           Victor job panel → PostgreSQL job queue → Worker process
           → Stream output → Store in DB → Render in panel

         AFTER (with session reports):
           Victor job panel → Read .borge-history/session-{id}.html

         The job panel becomes a list of links to session reports.
         No job queue table. No output streaming. No worker
         polling. No DB writes for content. Victor is a VIEWER
         for borge's committed output, not a storage engine.

         The PostgreSQL table for jobs shrinks to:
           id, session_path, status, started_at, completed_at
         The actual content lives in the repo.

         Victor can still trigger borge make / plan / drift via
         htmx POST, but it no longer needs to store the output.
         It waits for the commit, then reads the session report.

         See victor.borg section session-reports-consumption for
         the full victor-side spec.
        |))))
  (subsection brochure-integration
   (doc "How brochure renders build history as part of the public site")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Brochure walks .borge-history/ and includes it in the
         generated static site. The public gets a Build History
         page linked from the landing page. Each session is
         browsable with the same visual treatment as the rest
         of the brochure site.

         See brochure.borg section build-history for the full
         brochure-side spec.
        |))))))
